## Decoding the question
The question quotes **Mahmoud Fathalla**'s idea of the "**maternity death road**" (usually called the "**road to maternal death**"). **"Discuss women's health issues in the context of Pakistan's social, cultural and demographic situation"** asks you to **apply** his framework: show how each stage of the road is shaped by **social** (poverty, status), **cultural** (customs, norms) and **demographic** (population, age, rural–urban) factors in Pakistan. Use three headings for the three contexts.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) The idea
The Egyptian obstetrician **Mahmoud Fathalla** described maternal death as the **end of a long road** that begins long before pregnancy — with being born female and poor, undernourished, uneducated and married young. Most maternal deaths, he argued, are **preventable**; they reflect a society's neglect of women.

#### ii) The Pakistani picture
Pakistan's **maternal mortality ratio** was ==186== per 100,000 live births (**PMMS 2019**), far higher in rural areas and Balochistan.

#### iii) The argument
In Pakistan the road to maternal death is paved by **social** disadvantage, **cultural** practices and **demographic** pressures. Closing it requires social change as much as medical care.

### 2. Fathalla and the road
At the **Safe Motherhood Conference** in **Nairobi** (==1987==), which launched the global **Safe Motherhood Initiative**, Fathalla told the story of "**Mrs X**" and asked, "**Why did Mrs X die?**" He traced her death back through a chain of neglect. His often-quoted conclusion was that women are not dying of diseases we cannot treat, but because societies have yet to decide that their lives are worth saving. This shifted global thinking from purely medical solutions towards **women's rights and empowerment**.

### 3. The stages of the road
The road begins when a girl is **born female and poor** and fed less than her brothers; she grows **undernourished and anaemic**, receives **little schooling**, is **married young**, has **many pregnancies close together** without **family planning**, receives **no antenatal care**, and meets a **complication** — haemorrhage, eclampsia, obstructed labour or infection — without skilled help.

::: fig flow | The road to maternal death (after Fathalla, 1987).
@per 4
Born female and poor: fed and treated less
Undernourished and anaemic
Little or no schooling
Married young: adolescent pregnancy
Many close pregnancies; no family planning
No antenatal care: danger signs missed
Complication at birth
Delays in care: death
:::

### 4. The three delays
**Sereen Thaddeus and Deborah Maine** (==1994==) grouped the final stretch into **three delays**: (1) delay in **deciding** to seek care; (2) delay in **reaching** a facility; and (3) delay in **receiving** adequate care once there. Each delay has Pakistani causes, which the following sections trace through the social, cultural and demographic context.

### 5. The social context

#### i) Son preference and neglect of girls
Girls in many families receive less food and later medical care, so they reach adulthood undernourished — about ==42%== of women of reproductive age are **anaemic** (NNS 2018).

#### ii) Poverty
Poor families delay care because of the cost of **transport, medicines and private treatment**; most health spending in Pakistan is out of pocket.

#### iii) Low literacy
Female literacy is about ==53%== (Census 2023), far lower in rural areas; uneducated women know less about **danger signs** and **family planning**.

#### iv) Low decision-making power
Decisions about antenatal care and hospital delivery are often taken by **husbands and mothers-in-law** — the root of the **first delay**.

### 6. The cultural context

#### i) Early marriage
Many girls marry before 18. Only **Sindh** (==2013==) and **Islamabad** (==2025==) have set the minimum age at 18; the old federal age for girls is **16**. Adolescent mothers face higher risks.

#### ii) Purdah and mobility
Many women need a **male escort** or permission to travel to a clinic — adding to the first and second delays.

#### iii) Preference for female health workers
Many women will not be examined by a male doctor, but **female doctors** are scarce in rural areas, and many women medical graduates do not practise.

#### iv) Home births and taboos
**Home births** with traditional attendants (*dais*) are common; **taboos** on discussing pregnancy and contraception limit information; pressure to bear **sons** leads to repeated pregnancies.

### 7. The demographic context

#### i) Rapid growth
The **Census 2023** counted about ==241 million== people, growing at about **2.55%** a year. The **total fertility rate** was about ==3.6== and only ==34%== of married women used contraception (PDHS 2017–18).

#### ii) A young population
Large numbers of adolescent girls reach marriage age each year, so early marriage affects a very large number of young women.

#### iii) A rural majority and regional inequality
Most people live in rural areas where services are thinnest; **Balochistan** and parts of Sindh and Khyber Pakhtunkhwa have the worst indicators.

#### iv) Disasters
The ==2022== floods left about ==650,000== pregnant women in affected areas (UNFPA).

::: fig mapping | Pakistan's context and the stage of the road it shapes.
@left Pakistani context
@right Stage of the road
Son preference; poverty => Undernourished and anaemic
Low female literacy => Little knowledge or voice
Early marriage => Adolescent pregnancy
Pressure for sons; low contraception => Many close pregnancies
Purdah; family permission => Delay in deciding
Distance; roads; floods => Delay in reaching care
Few female staff; no blood banks => Delay in receiving care
:::

### 8. A Pakistani Mrs X
Imagine a girl in rural Balochistan: fed after her brothers, out of school because the nearest girls' school was far away, married at fifteen, five pregnancies in eight years, anaemic throughout, never seen by a doctor. When she bleeds after her sixth home delivery, the family waits for her husband to decide; the nearest hospital with a blood bank is four hours away. She dies on the road. Every stage is a point where **policy** could have saved her.

#### Why Fathalla's framing matters for policy
Because each stage of the road is shaped by society, policy can intervene at each one: girls' education and nutrition early in life, an enforced minimum marriage age of 18, family planning, antenatal care, skilled birth attendance and emergency transport. A health system that waits only for the final emergency acts too late; Fathalla's road shows where to act sooner.

### 9. Wider health issues along the road
The road does not only lead to maternal death. Beyond it, the same social and cultural factors shape other women's health issues: **anaemia and malnutrition**; **breast and cervical cancer** diagnosed late because of shame and lack of screening; **mental ill-health** linked to violence and isolation; and **domestic violence** itself.

### 10. Programmes
The **Lady Health Worker Programme** (==1994==), **community midwives**, the **Sehat Sahulat** health card, **BISP** and nutrition programmes such as **Benazir Nashonuma** address parts of the road — but coverage and quality are uneven, and each tackles only one stage of a road that needs to be closed at every point.

### 11. Closing the road
Enforce an **18-year marriage age** and keep girls in school; provide **iron and nutrition** support; widen **family planning** through LHWs and religious leaders; ensure **antenatal care** and **skilled birth attendance**; build **emergency obstetric care** with blood banks and ambulances; expand **female staff** and telemedicine; teach families the **danger signs**; and review every maternal death so that the system learns from each loss.

### 12. Conclusion
Fathalla's "road to maternal death" fits Pakistan closely. Its stages are shaped by **social** disadvantage (son preference, poverty, low literacy, low decision power), **cultural** practice (early marriage, purdah, home births, taboos) and **demographic** pressure (rapid growth, youth, rural majority, regional gaps). Because the road is built by society, society can close it — with education, nutrition, family planning, skilled care and women's empowerment.

## Why this answer scores
- Explains **Fathalla's idea** accurately (Nairobi 1987, "Mrs X", the Safe Motherhood Initiative).
- Applies it through the question's own three lenses — **social, cultural, demographic**.
- A **flow** figure, a **mapping** figure and a Pakistani "Mrs X" make the analysis vivid.
- Ends with how to **close the road** at every stage.
